1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,365
Laddoo Lab's six-pack was a hit.

2
00:00:02,365 --> 00:00:04,644
Every week, more families bought it.

3
00:00:04,944 --> 00:00:07,234
Then, this month, sales stopped growing.

4
00:00:07,784 --> 00:00:08,751
Zoya worries.

5
00:00:08,751 --> 00:00:10,165
Is her laddoo dying?

6
00:00:10,165 --> 00:00:11,065
No.

7
00:00:10,601 --> 00:00:12,166
It is just growing up.

8
00:00:12,816 --> 00:00:13,716
Think back.

9
00:00:13,660 --> 00:00:16,726
At first, only a few
neighbours knew the six-pack.

10
00:00:17,076 --> 00:00:17,976
Then Mrs.

11
00:00:17,921 --> 00:00:20,303
Rao told the other tiffin parents.

12
00:00:20,303 --> 00:00:22,148
Orders climbed every week.

13
00:00:22,498 --> 00:00:25,111
Now most families nearby already buy it.

14
00:00:25,111 --> 00:00:28,428
And a bakery has started
selling millet laddoos too.

15
00:00:28,778 --> 00:00:30,901
What Zoya is seeing has a name.

16
00:00:30,901 --> 00:00:32,470
The product life cycle.

17
00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:35,477
So what is the product life cycle?

18
00:00:35,777 --> 00:00:40,122
Kotler and Armstrong call it the
course of a product's sales and profits

19
00:00:40,122 --> 00:00:41,441
over its lifetime.

20
00:00:41,791 --> 00:00:47,292
Theodore Levitt made the idea famous
in the Harvard Business Review, in

21
00:00:47,292 --> 00:00:48,192
1965.

22
00:00:48,339 --> 00:00:51,654
Like a person, a product
goes through four stages.

23
00:00:51,654 --> 00:00:54,489
Introduction, growth,
maturity and decline.

24
00:00:55,139 --> 00:00:58,334
Let's watch the six-pack's
life, stage by stage.

25
00:00:58,684 --> 00:00:59,584
Stage one.

26
00:00:59,584 --> 00:01:00,577
Introduction.

27
00:01:00,577 --> 00:01:03,929
Few people know the
product, so sales are slow.

28
00:01:04,279 --> 00:01:05,488
Costs are high.

29
00:01:05,488 --> 00:01:08,325
Free tastings and Dev's
posters cost money.

30
00:01:08,325 --> 00:01:10,282
So there is no profit yet.

31
00:01:10,632 --> 00:01:11,532
Stage two.

32
00:01:11,503 --> 00:01:12,403
Growth.

33
00:01:12,164 --> 00:01:14,627
Word spreads, and sales climb fast.

34
00:01:14,977 --> 00:01:15,877
Mrs.

35
00:01:15,527 --> 00:01:17,189
Rao's friends join in.

36
00:01:17,189 --> 00:01:20,114
Profit turns positive, and keeps rising.

37
00:01:20,464 --> 00:01:21,431
Stage three.

38
00:01:21,431 --> 00:01:22,331
Maturity.

39
00:01:22,188 --> 00:01:25,565
Sales are at their highest,
but they stop growing.

40
00:01:25,915 --> 00:01:27,665
Most buyers already buy.

41
00:01:27,665 --> 00:01:30,750
Rivals copy you, and
profit starts to slip.

42
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:33,211
This is where Laddoo Lab is today.

43
00:01:33,561 --> 00:01:34,481
Stage four.

44
00:01:34,481 --> 00:01:35,381
Decline.

45
00:01:35,192 --> 00:01:39,134
Tastes change, or something
better arrives, and sales fall.

46
00:01:39,484 --> 00:01:40,947
Laddoo Lab isn't there.

47
00:01:40,947 --> 00:01:43,412
But if Zoya does nothing, it will be.

48
00:01:44,062 --> 00:01:46,035
Same product, four stages.

49
00:01:46,035 --> 00:01:48,498
Every stage needs a different plan.

50
00:01:49,198 --> 00:01:51,025
So what does each stage need?

51
00:01:51,375 --> 00:01:52,280
Introduction.

52
00:01:52,280 --> 00:01:54,902
Make people aware, and let them try it.

53
00:01:55,202 --> 00:01:56,102
Growth.

54
00:01:55,853 --> 00:01:58,939
Reach more buyers fast,
and keep quality high.

55
00:01:59,239 --> 00:02:00,139
Maturity.

56
00:01:59,999 --> 00:02:02,968
Defend your buyers, and
refresh the product.

57
00:02:03,268 --> 00:02:04,168
Decline.

58
00:02:03,967 --> 00:02:07,464
Cut costs, sell what is
left, or drop the product.

59
00:02:08,114 --> 00:02:12,426
Kotler and Armstrong give three
ways to refresh a mature product.

60
00:02:12,776 --> 00:02:14,268
Change the market.

61
00:02:14,268 --> 00:02:15,591
Find new users.

62
00:02:15,591 --> 00:02:18,294
Zoya offers the six-pack to gym-goers.

63
00:02:18,644 --> 00:02:19,924
Change the product.

64
00:02:19,924 --> 00:02:22,067
Nani starts testing a new flavour.

65
00:02:22,417 --> 00:02:23,634
Change the mix.

66
00:02:23,634 --> 00:02:27,657
Dev posts tiffin ideas with
the laddoos, to remind parents.

67
00:02:28,307 --> 00:02:30,274
Where do we see this at work today?

68
00:02:30,624 --> 00:02:31,631
Introduction.

69
00:02:31,631 --> 00:02:34,964
An electric scooter maker
offers free test rides.

70
00:02:35,314 --> 00:02:36,214
Growth.

71
00:02:35,981 --> 00:02:39,468
A food delivery app opens
in new cities every month.

72
00:02:39,818 --> 00:02:40,718
Maturity.

73
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:44,470
A toothpaste maker launches
new flavours to stay fresh.

74
00:02:44,820 --> 00:02:45,720
Decline.

75
00:02:45,654 --> 00:02:48,908
Music CDs faded as streaming apps arrived.

76
00:02:49,558 --> 00:02:52,160
So is the life cycle a perfect guide?

77
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:53,060
Not quite.

78
00:02:53,388 --> 00:02:54,410
The good side.

79
00:02:54,410 --> 00:02:57,166
It tells you what kind
of plan to make next.

80
00:02:57,516 --> 00:02:58,705
The hard side.

81
00:02:58,705 --> 00:03:01,576
You often see a stage only looking back.

82
00:03:01,576 --> 00:03:04,437
And stages can last months, or decades.

83
00:03:04,787 --> 00:03:07,915
Dev says sales are flat,
so drop the original.

84
00:03:07,915 --> 00:03:08,974
Nani says no.

85
00:03:08,974 --> 00:03:10,582
Families still love it.

86
00:03:10,932 --> 00:03:13,427
So Zoya keeps it, and plans something new.

87
00:03:13,777 --> 00:03:18,979
In 1976, Dhalla and Yuspeh warned in
the Harvard Business Review that the

88
00:03:18,979 --> 00:03:23,250
idea can make firms give up
on healthy products too soon.

89
00:03:23,600 --> 00:03:24,500
The lesson?

90
00:03:24,436 --> 00:03:27,345
Use the curve as a map,
not a death sentence.

91
00:03:27,995 --> 00:03:28,922
Quick check.

92
00:03:28,922 --> 00:03:31,923
Sales are high but flat,
and rivals copy you.

93
00:03:31,923 --> 00:03:33,251
Which stage is it?

94
00:03:35,151 --> 00:03:36,051
Maturity!

95
00:03:35,892 --> 00:03:39,392
Sales have peaked, and
competition is at its toughest.

96
00:03:39,992 --> 00:03:41,005
So, remember.

97
00:03:41,305 --> 00:03:45,930
Every product moves through four
stages: introduction, growth, maturity

98
00:03:45,930 --> 00:03:46,940
and decline.

99
00:03:47,290 --> 00:03:49,337
Each stage needs its own plan.

100
00:03:49,687 --> 00:03:54,168
And a mature product can be refreshed:
new users, a new product, or a new

101
00:03:54,168 --> 00:03:55,068
mix.

102
00:03:55,234 --> 00:03:59,718
Next time, we'll see how a new product
is born: new product development.
